{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790365591,"nonce":"LESLKbgYqdSvlPFbVyeCDbqnWeI=","title":"Lua and bytes without copies: buffers as views, read in place","message":"8013727 made Lua's bytes cross by copying, four times over: a Buffer entering Lua became a new Lua string (a Buffer passing through Lua was copied twice and came back a different object), a Lua string leaving where a Buffer is taken became a new Buffer, a plugin's string argument for a Buffer parameter became a new Buffer, and gpu's getMappedRange copied the mapping into a new Buffer. None of that is acceptable: data crosses as a view over its owner's storage, keeps its identity, and is copied only where the semantics demand, once.\n\nThe design:\n\n1. A Buffer entering Lua stays a Buffer: a foreign object (userdata) over the same bytes, and the same object when it leaves.\n2. Lua reads a Buffer in place with its own string library: string.unpack, string.byte, string.sub, string.len and # accept a Buffer and read its memory, through a byte-view hook on zyntax_embed::foreign (the embedder answers a pointer and length). A zyntax_lua change, landed through the zyntax queue.\n3. Readback is the one copy the semantics need, as Haxe (Bytes.alloc) and Wren (Float32Array.new) do it: Lua has no mutable byte type, so the core publishes caribou:Buffer with a size constructor, as it publishes Future; `local out = Buffer(16); readback:copyOut(0, out, 16); string.unpack(\"\u003cffff\", out)`.\n4. A Lua string where a Buffer is taken is a read-only Buffer over the string's own bytes, the string kept alive by it; so is a core string.\n5. The plugin ABI tells a buffer a plugin writes from one it reads (copyOut's out): a read-only buffer where a plugin writes is refused, rather than a plugin writing into a Lua string.\n6. gpu's getMappedRange goes: a copy invites this mistake, and a WebGPU view needs detach-on-unmap, which Haxe's and Wren's raw aliases cannot honour safely. copyOut is the readback.\n7. A Lua proxy releases its registry entry when it dies.","files":null}]}